    "C111", "C129"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a small, drug-like basic amine (dipropylaminoethyl-oxindole; MW 260, logP 2.85, TPSA 32.3, HBD 1). L4 records for structurally related basic amines demonstrate high intestinal absorption: imipramine human Fa ~100% and essentially complete absorption (C101, C104, C132, C150) with high passive permeability and low efflux (C145); procainamide human Fa 85% and 75–100% GI absorption (C102, C144, C146); PNU-96391 >90% GI absorption in rats (C133).",
      "card_ids": ["C101", "C104", "C132", "C150", "C145", "C102", "C144", "C146", "C133"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The most transferable L1 human absolute/systemic F outcomes for basic amines are all at or above the 20% threshold: PNU-96391 F=0.6 (C17), proterguride 62% (C11), psilocybin 52.7–55.0% (C04, C12), pridopidine high oral availability (C08), alpidem 32% (C03), tasimelteon 38% (C01, C05), and meptazinol in cirrhosis 27.9% (C07).",
      "card_ids": ["C17", "C11", "C04", "C12", "C08", "C03", "C01", "C05", "C07"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 provides no affirmative evidence of poor absorption or F < 20% for a relevant analog. Solubility of basic amines is adequate (zolmitriptan 1.70–9.79 mg/mL, C111, C129), and pH-dependent permeability of a basic compound is facile at small-intestinal pH 5.5–7.0 (C141).",
      "card_ids": ["C111", "C129", "C141"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The main unresolved component is first-pass metabolism/systemic clearance, not intestinal absorption; this uncertainty is expressed as evidence gaps and does not convert the favorable absorption evidence into a low prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C17", "C133"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C101", "C104", "C132", "C150", "C145", "C133", "C102", "C144", "C146", "C111", "C129", "C141"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No direct Caco-2/permeability or solubility measurement exists for the query itself; L4 records are for structurally related analogs.",
    "First-pass metabolism and systemic clearance of the query are not directly measured; F could be lower than absorption if first-pass is extensive.",
    "The query is fully ionized at physiological pH with logD 0.32, so formulation and dose effects on absorption are not directly tested.",
    "Species-dependent F for related molecules (e.g., alpidem 13% in male rat vs 32% in humans; proterguride 18% in monkey vs 62% in humans) limits quantitative transfer."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L4 intestinal absorption/permeability records for structurally related basic amines (imipramine Fa ~100%, procainamide 75–100%, PNU-96391 >90% absorption) support a favorable absorption component, and no L4 record provides affirmative evidence of F < 20%. Combined with transferable human F outcomes ≥20% for similar basic amines, the evidence supports F ≥ 20%; confidence is raised to high, with remaining first-pass uncertainty expressed as evidence gaps."
}

